# A machine with four processors: `cpu start` starts the other three, and
# work can then be spread over all of them.
@cpus 4
$ cpu
count: 4, 1 in use ('cpu start' starts the others)
$ cpu test
~ ^hashing 64 MiB on 1 processor: \d+ ms$
~ ^hashing 64 MiB on 1: \d+ ms$
~ ^same result; \d+\.\d times as fast$
$ cpu start
4 of 4 processors in use
$ cpu
count: 4, 4 in use
$ cpu start
4 of 4 processors in use
$ cpu test
~ ^hashing 64 MiB on 4: \d+ ms$
~ ^same result; \d+\.\d times as fast$
# Everything else still works with the others running: the self-tests
# (which spread a job over all four), files, a second window.
$ selftest
~ ^  ok    processors: every piece of a job runs exactly once$
~ ^selftest: \d+ passed, 0 failed$
$ echo still here > /x.txt
$ cat /x.txt
still here
$ cpu sideways
usage: cpu [start | test]
